// Value-range reasoning -- SOURCE program.
//
// rmm-zoo property: vr (Global transformations / Value range).
// Source: Moiseenko, Podkopaev & Koznov, "A Survey of Programming Language
//         Memory Models" (2021), Tables 1-2 -- the Global transformations group.
//         The zoo records vr = true for exactly three models: SC, Promising and
//         sMRD (Richards et al., OOPSLA 2025), which is MoRDor's own model.
//
// Value-range reasoning lets an optimiser narrow the set of values a read can
// return using facts the program guarantees, and then simplify branches that
// those facts decide. The de-facto constraint below -- the bracketed [ ... ]
// section, the same device litmus-tests/symmrd/alignment.lit uses -- states the
// implementation guarantee that x never holds a value outside 0..1.
//
// Under that guarantee the test (r1 < 2) is always true, so both arms of the
// branch write y := 1 and opt.lit collapses the branch entirely.
[x >= 0]
[x <= 1]
%%
x := 0;
y := 0;
r1 := x;
if (r1 < 2) {
  y := 1
} else {
  y := 2
}
